**Verdict:** trust intact, value high, recommendation **revise** — 0 blockers, 5 concerns. The draft's SHA matched `context.json` and the supplied hash before review.

**Strongest hit (FUTURE-H1/H5):** the evidence spine is a closed historical record — 1985 Wigg tapes, 1987 Dolezal interview, memoirs from 1994–2012 — so nothing in the argument can be overtaken by news, and the one current event (the Montreux gala) is used as ornament, never evidence. Remove it and the entire thesis stands. The Bohemian Rhapsody streaming claim is also a model of durable phrasing: it anchors the December 2018 *certification event* rather than a live count.

**Highest-priority concern (FUTURE-C1):** the gala passages are written in year-less future/present tense — "This September 5," "his estate is throwing the party," "the pretence gets its 80th birthday party," "will get its flowers" — and the event is September 5, 2026, eight days after the publication date. That's the shortest possible shelf life for the page's only decaying copy, and by mid-2027 "This September 5" points at the wrong birthday. The repair is mechanical: year-stamp the three passages and make the verbs event-survivable (or schedule a post-event tense pass). I confirmed via the [estate's on-sale page](https://www.freddiemercury.com/en/news/the-official-freddie-mercury-birthday-party-2026-on-sale-details) and [brianmay.com's August party guide](https://brianmay.com/queen-news/2026/08/the-official-freddie-mercury-birthday-party-2026-party-guide-3/) that the gala facts are accurate as written today — this is purely a durability issue, not an accuracy one.

The review includes a concrete twelve-month refresh list (gala tense conversion, the Nov 24, 2026 35th-anniversary coverage wave as a possible replacement anchor, the drifting 80-musicians/30-Fours corpus stat, and a standing check on Mary Austin's status before any future edit, since the ending's present tense depends on her).
